 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Performance:** H-2DGS achieves a compact representation of 7.97 MB across five real neurosurgical cases [S1, unofficial].
--   **Rendering Speed:** Interactive rendering at 29–42 ms per frame [S1, unofficial].
--   **Strengths:** Sharp, geometrically explicit surface; compact storage; supports postoperative deformation; integrates with MRI and tractography [S1, unofficial].
--   **Limitations:** Requires multi-view depth fusion; TSDF initialization depends on accurate depth rendering from Stage II; deformation may require careful tuning to preserve texture [S1, unofficial].
--   **When to Use:** Neurosurgical surface reconstruction where sharp, deformable surfaces are needed for integration with MRI and tractography, especially under limited view coverage [S1, unofficial].
--   **When Not to Use:** When a volumetric representation is required, or when real-time deformation is not needed and volumetric efficiency is prioritized over surface sharpness [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is interactive, taking 29–42 ms per frame for neurosurgical cases [S1, unofficial]. Training involves three stages, with Stage II adding overhead due to ray-wise quantile computation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Produces a compact representation, e.g., 7.97 MB for five neurosurgical cases [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Sharp geometric boundaries, deformable surface, compact representation, integration with MRI/tractography [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires multi-stage optimization, sensitive to initialization, and relies on TSDF fusion which may be noisy in sparse views [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When a sharp, deformable surface is required from volumetric data, especially in medical imaging with limited views [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When a purely volumetric representation is sufficient or when real-time training is required without multi-stage refinement [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-H-2DGS was introduced to address the limitations of conventional 3D Gaussian Splatting in neurosurgical visualization, where limited view coverage leads to geometric ambiguity. It builds on [3D Gaussian Splatting MVP](3410-3d-gaussian-splatting-mvp.md) by converting the volumetric representation into an explicit surface. The method was developed for neurosurgical planning and intraoperative navigation, where integration with MRI and tractography is critical [S1, unofficial].
+H-2DGS was introduced in "H-2DGS: Height-Field Gaussian Splatting for Sharp and Deformable Neurosurgical Surface Reconstruction" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of standard 3DGS in medical visualization, where depth ambiguity and volumetric thickness obscure internal structures [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on [3D Gaussian Splatting MVP](3410-3d-gaussian-splatting-mvp.md) by adding geometric constraints and a surface-oriented representation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Sources
